      "math_academy_id": "q-64978",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14219192/assets/634a863f2154398762af17da40b22d303b10b8846c90fa2021148779e1d1fa97.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=2{x}^{2}$ shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =c \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the equations $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\ge 0$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14219192/assets/844b92278c8b945a68104629b8dcb14d1b41c6875677ffda4da9615363e55221.png)",
